; =============================================================================
;  TFS 1.03  -  Acorn Teletext System ROM ("TELEROM"), for the BBC Microcomputer
;  Commented disassembly of TELEROMU.ROM (16K, &8000-&BFFF)
; =============================================================================
;
;  Generated by dis.py from teleromu.json + teleromu.ann. Labels and comments
;  are an analyst's reconstruction, not Acorn's original source.
;
;  WHAT THE ROM DOES
;  The ROM drives the Acorn Teletext Adapter, a box on the 1MHz bus with its
;  own tuner, an SAA5030 video input processor and SAA5020 timing chain, and
;  1K of RAM. The adapter has no acquisition chip (SAA5040 TAC): it just
;  stores every teletext line it finds in the field blanking interval. This
;  ROM does the rest in software, in the interrupt handler, and provides:
;    - OSWORD &7A: a teletext system call (fetch page, test arrived, transfer,
;      channel select, links, headers, ...)
;    - the Telesoft filing system (filing system 6): read-only, with
;      *PAGE *TRANSFER *DISPLAY *TIME *DATE *TUNE *CH1-4 *BBC1/2 *ITV1/2,
;      and Telesoftware download (*EXEC, *LOAD, *RUN) through a decoder for the
;      Telesoftware name/command format
;    - terminal mode (*TELETEXT): a language ROM that works as a teletext set
;      with function-key commands, kept pages, links and rolling headers
;
;  ADAPTER REGISTERS (page &FC)
;    &FC10 read   status: b7 INT, b6 DOR (overrun), b5 DEW, b4 FSYN,
;                 b3-b0 option links (all 0 when the adapter has no power)
;    &FC10 write  control latch: b0-b1 channel, b2 TTXEN (capture enable),
;                 b3 IRQ enable, b4 AFC on, b5 spare (shadow copy in &0E8E)
;    &FC11 write  select one of the 16 64-byte line slots in the adapter RAM
;    &FC12 r/w    read/write the selected slot; the address steps on each access
;    &FC13 write  STATCLR: clear the INT and DOR flip-flops
;
;  MEMORY USE
;    &0E00-&19FF  static workspace (shared by filing systems; stashed into the
;                 adapter RAM and private workspace when another FS takes over)
;      &0E00-&0E14  interrupt scratch: decoded header, current row/magazine
;      &0E15-&0E1C  page being captured in each magazine (&FF = none)
;      &0E5C-&0E83  broadcast service data packet (magazine 8 row 30)
;      &0E8E        control latch shadow
;      &0EA0-&0F99  25 page request slots of 10 bytes
;      &0F9A-&1599  Telesoftware name tables (3 lone + 3 escaped, 256 bytes each)
;      &159A-&1619  Telesoftware decoder frame stack
;      &161A-&1699  Telesoftware decoder state
;      &169A-&1999  Telesoftware heap (grows up) and output queue (grows down)
;    private workspace: 8 pages; slot 0's page buffer while TFS is selected,
;                   the stashed workspace while it isn't
;    terminal mode: page buffers from PAGE upwards (&602 bytes each),
;                   page copy at &7800, MODE 7 screen at &7C00
;
;  DIFFERENCES FROM TFS103.ROM (a corrupt dump of the same ROM)
;    &8000   &4C here (JMP terminal_entry); &00 in TFS103.ROM, which made
;            *TELETEXT crash into BRK
;    &A000   &91 here (STA (&B4),Y); &00 in TFS103.ROM, which broke the end
;            of OSFILE load
;    &BD2E-  zeros here; left-over code from another build in TFS103.ROM
;
;  CONVENTIONS
;    &xx       hexadecimal, as in BBC BASIC
;    Lxxxx     an address with no better name
;    "slot"    either a page request slot (&0EA0) or an adapter RAM line
;              slot (&FC11); the comments say which
;
